{
  "format": "phaenon.json as its readers answer it (../design/tooling.md §3.7, T19): the compiler reading a project's own file (readProject in cmd/phn/project.go), the compiler reading an installed package's (readInstalledProject), and the Composer plugin, which reads installed packages alone (composer/src/ProjectFile.php). \"file\" is the project file's text. Where the file is not text, \"bytes\" holds it in base64. \"refused\" marks a file that is in error for all three, and \"error\" is then part of the compiler's error, where the vector holds it to one. Otherwise \"roots\" maps each source root to its output directory and \"committed\" lists the roots whose outputs the project commits, in the file's order, all as cleaned paths with forward slashes. \"root\" marks a file that is in error as a project's own, with part of that error, and is read as given as an installed package's: it has keys the compiler does not know, which \"unknown\" lists, sorted, as both readers of an installed package name them. A file without \"root\" is read alike in both ways, and has no unknown key. \"keys\" are the keys of the file's object that the readers know, and \"keys-of-unused\" those under \"unused\": both readers are held to these lists, so that a key one of them learns is one the other learns. The two readers decode with different libraries, and the compiler refuses by name what PHP's decoder refuses and Go's would read: bytes that are no UTF-8, half a surrogate pair, a key that begins with U+0000, nesting past 511 values. No file is known that one reads and the other refuses; should one turn up, the plugin leaves its package to the compiler, which then does for it all the plugin did not.",
  "keys": [
    "roots",
    "committed",
    "exclude",
    "php",
    "fmt",
    "unused"
  ],
  "keys-of-unused": [
    "reflectedAttributes"
  ],
